nt \u2014 forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning. The phases are not sequential, because groups can cycle through the phases.</br></br>Stage 1: In the forming stage, it was said by Tuckman that team members will look to the team leader for guidance and direction. Being accepted in a group is challenging so often members will gather information about the similarities and differences among themselves. Members attempt to become oriented to the tasks, as well as to one another.", "id": 1, "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "32000", "text": "Stage 2:  Having been characterized by competition and conflict, the next stage is known as storming. As team members attempt to organize for the task, it has been discovered by Tuckman that questions arise among the group. These questions will result in conflicts over leadership, structure, power, and authority.</br></br>Stage 3: The norming stage is characterized by members willing to change their preconceived ideas or opinions on the basis of facts presented by other members. Leadership is shared, and cliques dissolve. Having been accepted by the group, members begin to identify with one another. The level of trust in each other contributes to the development of group cohesion. It has been agreed to share feelings and ideas, solicit and give feedback to one another, and explore actions related to the task.", "id": 2, "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "80000", "text": "Stage 4: The performing stage is not reached by all groups. In this stage, members can work independently, in subgroups, or as a total unit. Their roles dynamically adjust to the changing needs of the group and individuals. It is decided to support each other when solving problems and put an emphasis on achievement. The overall goal is productivity through problem solving and work.</br></br>Stage 5: The final stage, adjourning was proposed by Tuckman to involve the termination of tasks and disengagement from relationships. Being given the opportunity, members say their personal goodbyes to each other.  Having been recognized for their participation and achievement, the team is dismembered.", "id": 3, "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le06ob09re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le06ob09re2img01.jpg"}}], "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": [], "title": ""}]}]}